In mathematics, binary relations (relations between two sets) are classified based on their domain/codomain types, their structural properties on a single set, and their special functional roles. [1, 2, 3, 4] 
Here is the complete, comprehensive classification of all mathematical relations, categorized by their formal properties, without omitting any distinct logical category.
------------------------------
## 1. Relations Based on Set Mappings
These types are defined by how elements from a domain set $A$ relate to elements in a codomain set $B$. [5, 6] 

* Empty Relation: No element from set $A$ is related to any element in set $B$ ($\mathcal{R} = \emptyset$).
* Universal (Full) Relation: Every single element in set $A$ is related to every single element in set $B$ ($\mathcal{R} = A \times B$).
* Identity Relation: An element is related exclusively to itself ($I_A = \{(a, a) \mid a \in A\}$).
* Inverse Relation: The flipped orientation of an existing relation ($\mathcal{R}^{-1} = \{(b, a) \mid (a, b) \in \mathcal{R}\}$). [7, 8, 9, 10, 11] 

------------------------------
## 2. Structural Properties on a Single Set (Endorelations)
When a relation maps a set to itself ($\mathcal{R} \subseteq A \times A$), it must satisfy or reject specific logical conditions for all elements $a, b, c \in A$.
## Reflexivity Properties

* Reflexive: Every element relates to itself ($\forall a, a\mathcal{R}a$).
* Irreflexive (Anti-reflexive): No element relates to itself ($\forall a, \neg(a\mathcal{R}a)$).
* Non-reflexive: A relation that is neither fully reflexive nor fully irreflexive. [12, 13, 14, 15, 16] 

## Symmetry Properties

* Symmetric: If $a$ relates to $b$, then $b$ must relate to $a$ ($\forall a,b, a\mathcal{R}b \implies b\mathcal{R}a$).
* Asymmetric: If $a$ relates to $b$, then $b$ cannot relate to $a$ ($\forall a,b, a\mathcal{R}b \implies \neg(b\mathcal{R}a)$).
* Antisymmetric: If $a$ relates to $b$ and $b$ relates to $a$, they must be the exact same element ($\forall a,b, (a\mathcal{R}b \land b\mathcal{R}a) \implies a = b$).
* Non-symmetric: A relation that doesn't follow strict symmetric, asymmetric, or antisymmetric rules. [17, 18, 19, 20, 21] 

## Transitivity Properties

* Transitive: If $a$ relates to $b$ and $b$ relates to $c$, then $a$ relates to $c$ ($\forall a,b,c, (a\mathcal{R}b \land b\mathcal{R}c) \implies a\mathcal{R}c$).
* Intransitive: A relation where transitivity fails everywhere ($\forall a,b,c, (a\mathcal{R}b \land b\mathcal{R}c) \implies \neg(a\mathcal{R}c)$).
* Non-transitive: A general relation where transitivity holds for some element paths but fails for others. [22, 23, 24, 25, 26] 

## Connectedness Properties

* Connected (Total): For any two distinct elements, $a$ must relate to $b$ or $b$ must relate to $a$ ($\forall a,b \, (a \neq b), a\mathcal{R}b \lor b\mathcal{R}a$).
* Strongly Connected: Every pair of elements is related, including elements to themselves ($\forall a,b, a\mathcal{R}b \lor b\mathcal{R}a$). [27, 28, 29, 30, 31] 

------------------------------
## 3. Composite Structural Relations
Mathematicians group the primary properties above into major compound classifications to structure algebra, calculus, and set theory:
## Equivalence-Style Relations

* Equivalence Relation: A relation that is simultaneously Reflexive, Symmetric, and Transitive (e.g., $a \equiv b \pmod n$).
* Tolerance (Compatibility) Relation: A relation that is Reflexive and Symmetric but not necessarily Transitive. [32, 33, 34, 35, 36] 

## Order-Style Relations

* Preorder (Quasiorder): A relation that is Reflexive and Transitive.
* Partial Order: A relation that is Reflexive, Antisymmetric, and Transitive (e.g., subset inclusion $\subseteq$).
* Strict Partial Order: A relation that is Irreflexive, Asymmetric, and Transitive (e.g., strictly less than $<$).
* Total (Linear) Order: A Partial Order that is also Connected/Total (e.g., $\le$ on real numbers).
* Strict Total Order: A Strict Partial Order that is also Connected/Total (e.g., $<$ on real numbers).
* Well-Order: A Total Order where every non-empty subset contains a unique least element (e.g., $\le$ on natural numbers $\mathbb{N}$). [37, 38, 39, 40, 41] 

------------------------------
## 4. Uniquely Functional & Higher-Arity Relations

* Functional Relation (Right-Unique): If an element relates to two items, those items must be identical ($a\mathcal{R}b \land a\mathcal{R}c \implies b = c$). This forms the basis of mathematical functions. [42, 43, 44, 45] 
* Serial (Left-Total) Relation: Every element in the domain has at least one matching element in the codomain ($\forall a \exists b, a\mathcal{R}b$). [46, 47] 
* Bijective Relation: A relation that is left-total, right-total, left-unique, and right-unique (a perfect one-to-one match). [48] 
* Finitary ($n$-ary) Relation: A relation spanning across more than two sets ($A_1 \times A_2 \times \dots \times A_n$). This includes Ternary relations ($n=3$) and Quaternary relations ($n=4$). [49, 50, 51] 

